<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Limb II · ix</p>
  <h1>Runes, Charms &amp; Natural Energies</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">An alphabet, a grammar of small made things, and a word — <em>energy</em> — that has
  been so thoroughly abused that we had better define it before we use it once.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================== OPENING ================================ -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Begin Here</p>
    <h2>They Were Letters</h2>
    <p class="lede">The single most important fact about the runes is the one most often left out: the Elder
    Futhark is a writing system, used to write ordinary things.</p>

    <p>It appears in the second century CE across Germanic-speaking Europe, almost certainly adapted from an
    Old Italic alphabet, and the surviving corpus — some four hundred-odd Elder Futhark inscriptions — is
    mostly names. Maker's marks. Ownership tags. "Hariso made this." A comb inscribed with the word
    <em>comb</em>. ◆ Scholarship, and not a disappointment: the shapes are angular because they were cut
    across the grain of wood and into stone, which is also why so few of them have horizontal strokes.</p>

    <p>Some inscriptions <em>are</em> charms, and plainly so — the Lindholm amulet, the Kragehul spear-shaft,
    bracteates carrying the word <em>alu</em>. So the magical use is genuine and attested. ◆ But the modern
    system of twenty-four meanings, elemental correspondences, reversed readings and three-rune spreads is a
    twentieth-century construction, largely from Guido von List's invented Armanen set of 1908 and from the
    divinatory tarot it borrowed its shape from. The order uses that system. It simply refuses to lie about
    where it came from.</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">◆ established</span> ·
    <span class="hist">◆ scholarship and history</span> · <span class="spec">◆ speculative</span> ·
    <span class="myth">◆ myth and tradition</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

