  <p class="muted small wrap narrow" style="margin-top:1.6rem">The three groups of eight — the
  <em>ættir</em> — are attested on the Kylver stone (c. 400 CE) and the Vadstena bracteate, so the ordering
  is real and ancient. The <em>names</em>, however, are reconstructed backwards from the much later
  Anglo-Saxon, Norwegian and Icelandic rune poems. ◆ We do not have a Proto-Germanic speaker telling us what
  they called the fourteenth rune. <em>Perthro</em> is a scholarly guess at a word nobody is sure of.</p>
</section>

