<!-- ============================ PRACTICE ================================= -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Practice</p>
    <h2>Four Honest Workings</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card reveal">
      <div class="card-glyph">{% glyph "seed" %}</div>
      <h3>Cut a Set</h3>
      <p>Twenty-four pieces of a fallen branch, one rune each, cut rather than drawn — the tradition is
      emphatic that the stave is <em>carved</em>. You will learn the shapes properly in an afternoon because
      your hands will, and you will never again mistake Algiz for Eihwaz. Leave the twenty-fifth blank piece
      out of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card reveal">
      <div class="card-glyph">{% glyph "eye" %}</div>
      <h3>Read Three, Honestly</h3>
      <p>Situation, obstacle, what is actually being asked of you. Draw, then say the reading aloud before
      you consult any book. What the staves reliably do is <em>externalise</em> — they give a stalled mind a
      random prompt and permission to say the thing it already knew. ◆ That mechanism is real and is enough.
      Do not use them for medical or legal decisions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card reveal">
      <div class="card-glyph">{% glyph "tree" %}</div>
      <h3>One Charm, Properly Made</h3>
      <p>All five components from Plate III: a material you gathered, a line of words you wrote, a cutting or
      knotting act, a chosen hour, and a carrier you will actually keep on you. Make it for one specific
      thing. Then — this is the part people skip — retire it when the thing is done, and say so.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card reveal">
      <div class="card-glyph">{% glyph "spiral" %}</div>
      <h3>The Measurable Energies</h3>
      <p>Twenty minutes of morning light in the eyes within an hour of waking; two hours a week under trees;
      bare feet on cold ground not because of electrons but because of the shock and the attention it
      compels. ◆ These are the strongest interventions on this page and the least glamorous, which is the
      usual arrangement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== LEDGER ================================= -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Holds Up</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> The Elder Futhark is a genuine second-century script with a fixed, attested order in three ættir.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Charm and amulet use of runes is real and documented — Lindholm, Kragehul, the bracteates, <em>alu</em>.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> The five-part charm grammar recurs independently across unrelated cultures.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Randomised prompts genuinely help stalled decision-making by forcing articulation.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Carried objects function as effective commitment cues under stress.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Forest exposure, daylight timing, cold and heat all have real, measured physiological effects.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Does Not</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>The Armanen futhark.</strong> Eighteen runes received in a vision in 1902. Not ancient, and politically poisoned at the root.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>The blank rune.</strong> Introduced in 1982. Appears in no historical set.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Reversed meanings.</strong> Imported from tarot; several staves are symmetrical and cannot reverse at all.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Ley lines as energy currents.</strong> Alfred Watkins proposed sightlines of old tracks in 1921; the energy reading arrives in the 1960s and nothing has ever been detected.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Orgone.</strong> No instrument has found it in eighty years.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Earthing claims.</strong> The studies are small, unblinded and largely funded by people selling mats.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>That any rune is unlucky.</strong> Hagalaz means hail. Hail is weather.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== TIMELINE =============================== -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>A Line Through It</h2>
      <ul class="timeline">
        <li><span class="when">c. 150 CE</span> The Vimose comb — among the earliest datable runic objects. It says <em>harja</em>, probably a name.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 400</span> The Kylver stone preserves the full futhark in order; the ættir are confirmed.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 500</span> The Lindholm amulet: unambiguous charm use, including a long string of repeated staves.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 800</span> The Younger Futhark reduces to sixteen staves as the language changes — fewer letters, more ambiguity.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 1000</span> Anglo-Saxon futhorc expands to thirty-three; the rune poems begin to fix names.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 1300</span> Codex Runicus — an entire legal code in runes, long after Christianisation. Letters to the end.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1639</span> Denmark bans runic writing as sorcery; the script's magical reputation is partly a product of its suppression.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1908</span> Von List publishes the invented Armanen set.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1933–45</span> The staves are conscripted by the SS; Sowilo, Algiz, Tiwaz and Odal are turned into insignia.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1982</span> Ralph Blum's <em>Book of Runes</em>; divination as practised today is largely downstream of it.</li>
        <li><span class="when">now</span> Careful scholarship and careless commerce run in parallel. Tell them apart by whether the source names its sources.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Reading</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>R. I. Page</strong>, <em>Runes</em> (British Museum, 1987) — short, authoritative, bracingly unimpressed by mysticism.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Terje Spurkland</strong>, <em>Norwegian Runes and Runic Inscriptions</em> (2005) — what the stones actually say.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Nicholas Goodrick-Clarke</strong>, <em>The Occult Roots of Nazism</em> (1985) — the essential, careful account of von List, Wiligut and the Ariosophists.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Owen Davies</strong>, <em>Popular Magic: Cunning-folk in English History</em> (2003) — charms as they were really commissioned and paid for.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Qing Li</strong>, <em>Shinrin-Yoku</em> (2018) — the forest-bathing research, read with attention to effect sizes.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>The Hávamál</strong>, stanzas 138–164 — Odin on the tree, and the eighteen charms. Primary myth, thirteenth-century manuscript.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

